<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  The McDonnell F-4C Phantom II, for JSBSim v1.3.3. FSG's own aircraft file: new in FSG, milestone 3
  (docs/milestones/3.md, pull request 3). Nothing here is copied from another F-4 model. JSBSim's own files
  (aircraft/f16/f16.xml, aircraft/c172p/c172p.xml) were read for the format only.

  So far (pull request 3): weight, balance and inertias; the wheels and the airframe's contact points; two
  J79-GE-15 engines with their afterburners and the throttle system that lights them. There is no
  aerodynamics yet: it stands and runs its engines, and that is all. Aerodynamics, flight controls, flaps
  and speedbrakes come in pull requests 5 to 7.

  Where each number comes from. Every number below names one of these.
    CR-2144   NASA CR-2144, Heffley and Jewell, "Aircraft Handling Qualities Data", 1972, section IV (the
              F-4C): table IV-2 (geometry, weight, inertias, thrust line) and figure IV-2.
              https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19730003312
    T.O.      T.O. 1F-4C-1, the F-4C flight manual (1 October 1970), section I.
    Wings     135crewdog/wings, src/sim/cards/f4c.js, commit 6d689b7: where the owner's model
              (models/f4c.glb) has its wheels, its lowest points and its engine exits, measured from the
              model in feet from its origin, which is the centre of gravity.
    Reasoned  an engineering choice with no source; the comment says what would settle it.

  Axes. JSBSim's structural frame, in inches: x aft, y to the right, z up. Its origin is the centre of
  gravity as loaded, which is the model's origin, so a point "19.75 ft ahead, 6.4 ft below" is
  x = -237, z = -76.8. The aerodynamic reference point and the visual reference point are there too.
-->
<fdm_config name="F-4C Phantom II" version="2.0" release="ALPHA"
 xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
 xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="http://jsbsim.sourceforge.net/JSBSim.xsd">

