 <!--
  CR-2144, table IV-2: 38,924 lb, with roll, pitch and yaw inertias of 25,002, 122,193 and 139,767 slug-ft2
  and a cross inertia Ixz of 2,177 slug-ft2, in body axes (clean, about 60% internal fuel).

  The weight is made of two parts so that a test can fly another weight by changing the fuel: 30,000 lb
  here and 8,924 lb in the one tank (see the propulsion section). Both are at the centre of gravity, and a
  tank with no size has no inertia of its own, so the inertias written here are the loaded ones.
  tests/f4c.test.js reads the weight, the centre of gravity and all four inertias back from a run.

  The sign of Ixz. CR-2144's is the usual one: positive when the airplane's long axis of mass points nose
  down, as the F-4's does under its tall fin. JSBSim v1.3.3 (src/models/FGMassBalance.cpp,
  ReadInertiaMatrix) by default reads <ixz> as the opposite of the usual one: it puts the number as written
  into the inertia matrix's corner, where the usual convention has minus Ixz. With
  negated_crossproduct_inertia="false" it reads the usual one, and the property inertia/ixz-slugs_ft2 then
  reads back +2,177. (JSBSim's own f16.xml takes the other way round: the default, with -982 written.)
 -->
 <mass_balance negated_crossproduct_inertia="false">
  <ixx unit="SLUG*FT2"> 25002 </ixx>
  <iyy unit="SLUG*FT2"> 122193 </iyy>
  <izz unit="SLUG*FT2"> 139767 </izz>
  <ixz unit="SLUG*FT2"> 2177 </ixz>
  <emptywt unit="LBS"> 30000 </emptywt>
  <location name="CG" unit="IN">
   <x> 0 </x>
   <y> 0 </y>
   <z> 0 </z>
  </location>
 </mass_balance>

 <!--
  The wheels. Wings: with the airplane standing, the tyres meet the runway 6.4 ft below the centre of
  gravity: the nose wheel 19.75 ft ahead of it, the main wheels 3.52 ft behind and 8.96 ft out. Standing
  on those, the nose wheel carries 3.52 / 23.27 = 15.1% of the weight: 5,888 lb, and 16,518 lb on each main.

  JSBSim's location is the tyre's lowest point with the leg unloaded, so each is written lower than where
  it stands by what that leg gives under its load:
    main legs  33,000 lb/ft: 16,518 lb is 0.5005 ft (6.01 in), so z = -76.8 - 6.01 = -82.81
    nose leg   15,000 lb/ft:  5,888 lb is 0.3925 ft (4.71 in), so z = -76.8 - 4.71 = -81.51
  tests/f4c.test.js stands it and measures each tyre within 1 in of Wings' place, and the airplane level.

  Reasoned, all of it engineering choice with no F-4 source: the stiffness (the airplane bounces on its
  legs a little over once a second), the damping (about half of critical: 4,100 and 1,650), and the
  friction (JSBSim's usual tyre numbers, as its F-16 and Cessna have: 0.8 braked, 0.5 sliding sideways,
  0.02 rolling). A landing that feels wrong on the phone would settle them (milestone 3, pull request 7).

  The nosewheel steers 30 degrees each way. The real jet's nose gear steering turns it about 70 degrees
  (not checked against the flight manual), which is for parking; 30 is what a pilot can use at a taxi: a
  turn 80 ft across. Reasoned; pull request 7 taxis it. The main wheels have the brakes, left and right.
  All three legs retract.
 -->
